    "summary": "What log_b c means, evaluating logarithms with and without technology, the common logarithm, and logarithmic scales."
  },
  "framework": null,
  "contentRevision": "bb809ff6c43d1ae1",
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q01",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "meaning",
      "difficulty": "Quick check",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "Which equation is equivalent to log₆ 216 = 3?",
      "choices": [
        "3⁶ = 216",
        "6³ = 216",
        "216³ = 6",
        "6 · 3 = 216"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 1,
      "explanation": "log_b c = a if and only if bᵃ = c. So log₆ 216 = 3 means 6³ = 216.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "The base is 6, so 6 is raised to the power 3, not 3 to the power 6.",
        "bᵃ = c with b = 6, a = 3, c = 216.",
        "This raises the argument to the power instead of the base.",
        "A logarithm is an exponent, not a factor."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 0,
      "distinguisher": "The base of the logarithm is the base of the power.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q02",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "meaning",
      "difficulty": "Quick check",
      "skill": "solve",
      "prompt": "What is the value of log₂ (1/16)?",
      "choices": [
        "4",
        "1/4",
        "−1/4",
        "−4"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 3,
      "explanation": "2⁻⁴ = 1/16, so log₂ (1/16) = −4.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "2⁴ = 16, not 1/16.",
        "2^(1/4) is the fourth root of 2, not 1/16.",
        "2^(−1/4) is about 0.841, not 1/16.",
        "The reciprocal of 2⁴ is 2⁻⁴ = 1/16."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 0,
      "distinguisher": "A reciprocal means a negative exponent, not a fractional one.",
      "skillLabel": "Solve equations and inequalities",
      "skillCode": "1.A"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q03",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "meaning",
      "difficulty": "Quick check",
      "skill": "solve",
      "prompt": "What is the value of log 1000?",
      "choices": [
        "3",
        "100",
        "10",
        "1/3"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 0,
      "explanation": "With no base written, log means the common logarithm with base 10. Since 10³ = 1000, log 1000 = 3.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "10³ = 1000.",
        "100 is 1000/10, not an exponent.",
        "10 is the base of the common logarithm, not the value of log 1000.",
        "1000^(1/3) = 10, which reverses the question."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 2,
      "distinguisher": "Unwritten base means base 10; the answer is the exponent.",
      "skillLabel": "Solve equations and inequalities",
      "skillCode": "1.A"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q04",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "log-scale",
      "difficulty": "Quick check",
      "skill": "describe",
      "prompt": "On a base-10 logarithmic scale, two marks are 3 units apart. How do the values at those marks compare?",
      "choices": [
        "The larger value is 3 more than the smaller value.",
        "The larger value is 30 times the smaller value.",
        "The larger value is 1000 times the smaller value.",
        "The larger value is 3 times the smaller value."
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 2,
      "explanation": "Each unit represents a multiplicative change by the base, 10. Three units represent 10³ = 1000.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "A difference of 3 describes a standard scale.",
        "30 = 3·10 adds the factors instead of multiplying them.",
        "Three factors of 10 give 10³ = 1000.",
        "The base of the scale is 10, not 3."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 1,
      "distinguisher": "Each unit multiplies by the base; units compound.",
      "skillLabel": "Describe function characteristics",
      "skillCode": "3.A"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q05",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "evaluating",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "solve",
      "prompt": "What is the exact value of log₂₇ 9?",
      "choices": [
        "3",
        "2/3",
        "1/3",
        "3/2"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 1,
      "explanation": "27 = 3³ and 9 = 3². If 27ᵃ = 9, then 3^(3a) = 3², so 3a = 2 and a = 2/3.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "27 is 9·3, but logarithms are exponents, not quotients.",
        "(3³)ᵃ = 3² gives a = 2/3.",
        "27^(1/3) = 3, not 9.",
        "3/2 is log₉ 27, with the base and argument switched."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 3,
      "distinguisher": "Write both numbers as powers of 3 and compare exponents.",
      "skillLabel": "Solve equations and inequalities",
      "skillCode": "1.A"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q06",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "evaluating",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "identify-information",
      "prompt": "Between which two consecutive integers is log₃ 50?",
      "choices": [
        "1 and 2",
        "2 and 3",
        "4 and 5",
        "3 and 4"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 3,
      "explanation": "3³ = 27 < 50 < 81 = 3⁴, so 3 < log₃ 50 < 4. Technology gives log₃ 50 ≈ 3.561.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "3² = 9 is far less than 50.",
        "3³ = 27 is still less than 50.",
        "3⁴ = 81 is already greater than 50.",
        "27 < 50 < 81 places log₃ 50 between 3 and 4."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 1,
      "distinguisher": "Bracket the argument between consecutive powers of the base.",
      "skillLabel": "Identify information in a representation",
      "skillCode": "2.A"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q07",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "meaning",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "describe",
      "prompt": "For which value of b is log_b 1 = 0 true?",
      "choices": [
        "For every b > 0 with b ≠ 1",
        "Only for b = 10",
        "Only for b = 1",
        "For no value of b"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 0,
      "explanation": "b⁰ = 1 for every allowed base b, so log_b 1 = 0 whenever b > 0 and b ≠ 1.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "Any allowed base raised to 0 is 1.",
        "It is true for base 10, but also for every other allowed base.",
        "b = 1 is not an allowed base for a logarithm.",
        "b⁰ = 1 makes the statement true."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 1,
      "distinguisher": "b⁰ = 1 for every allowed base.",
      "skillLabel": "Describe function characteristics",
      "skillCode": "3.A"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q08",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "evaluating",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "solve",
      "prompt": "Which of the following is closest to log 40?",
      "choices": [
        "4",
        "0.602",
        "1.602",
        "2.602"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 2,
      "explanation": "10¹ < 40 < 10², so log 40 is between 1 and 2. Technology gives log 40 ≈ 1.602.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "10⁴ = 10,000, not 40.",
        "0.602 ≈ log 4, which is log 40 − 1.",
        "log 40 ≈ 1.602.",
        "2.602 ≈ log 400."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 1,
      "distinguisher": "Check the integer part first: 40 is between 10 and 100.",
      "skillLabel": "Solve equations and inequalities",
      "skillCode": "1.A"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q09",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "log-scale",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "apply-results",
      "prompt": "A quantity is plotted at position 2 on a base-10 logarithmic scale, and a second quantity is plotted at position 5. How many times as large is the second quantity as the first?",
      "choices": [
        "3",
        "2.5",
        "300",
        "1000"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 3,
      "explanation": "The positions differ by 3 units, and each unit is a factor of 10. So the second quantity is 10³ = 1000 times as large: 10⁵/10² = 1000.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "3 is the difference in positions, not the factor between the values.",
        "5/2 divides the positions instead of comparing 10⁵ and 10².",
        "300 multiplies the difference by 100 instead of using 10³.",
        "10⁵/10² = 10³ = 1000."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 0,
      "distinguisher": "A difference of positions is an exponent of the base.",
      "skillLabel": "Apply results in context",
      "skillCode": "3.B"
    },
    {
      "id": "precalculus-2-9-q10",
      "topicId": "precalculus-2-9",
      "concept": "meaning",
      "difficulty": "Core",
      "skill": "equivalent-forms",
      "prompt": "If 5ᵗ = 12, which of the following is equal to t?",
      "choices": [
        "log₁₂ 5",
        "log₅ 12",
        "12/5",
        "log 12 − log 5"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 1,
      "explanation": "bᵃ = c means log_b c = a. With b = 5 and c = 12, t = log₅ 12.",
      "sourceIds": [
        "ced",
        "ced-topic-2-9"
      ],
      "choiceExplanations": [
        "The base of the logarithm must be the base of the power, 5.",
        "5ᵗ = 12 means t = log₅ 12.",
        "12/5 = 2.4, but 5^2.4 ≈ 47.6, not 12.",
        "log 12 − log 5 = log 2.4, which is not log₅ 12."
      ],
      "nearMissIndex": 0,
      "distinguisher": "Rewrite with the same base as the power.",
      "skillLabel": "Rewrite in equivalent forms",
      "skillCode": "1.B"
    }
  ],
